etymology:
{{etymon|sv|:der|non:Alfarr<ety:af<alfr><herr>>}}
{{der|sv|non|Alfarr}}, from {{compound|non|alfr|t1=elf|herr|t2=army|nocat=1}}.
* Revived in the 19th century, partly because it was mistaken for a translation of German [[Ernst]] from Swedish {{m|sv|allvar||seriousness}}.

Name sense: Ultimately {{glossary|genitive}} form of {{m|sv|Arvid}}, later transformed.

Locality sense: Ultimately named after {{w|Arild (helgon)|Saint Arild|lang=sv}}. First known as {{bor|sv|da|Hellij Aruitz leije||Holy Arild's Fishing Village}} in 1524, and {{bor|sv|da|hellige Arrildz leye}} in 1556.

Up to late 19th century locally known as {{m|sv|Arildsläge}}; {{surf|sv|Arilds|läge|t1=Arild's|t2=fishing village||nocap=1|nocat=1}}, or shortened to {{m|sv|Läget||the fishing village}}. During late 19th century officially named {{m-self|sv|Arild}}.{{ref|[https://www.isof.se/lar-dig-mer/publikationer/publikationer/2016-01-01-svenskt-ortnamnslexikon Svenskt ortnamnslexikon (2016)], pages 24–25, by [https://www.isof.se/ Swedish Institute for Language and Folklore] and [https://www.nordiska.uu.se/ Uppsala University Department of Scandinavian Languages]}}
